PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The accumulating discussion has clarified that the shared element functions as a culturally embedded referential or idiomatic marker (a symbolic "mark" whose double meaning is locked to English phrasing and cultural assumptions) rather than a pure phonetic overlap. This one-off plot device, atypical for both authors, creates a barrier of resonance and recognition that literal transfer cannot preserve.  

I succeeds by crafting a functionally parallel marker that still drives the deduction and reads as native. III eliminates the mismatch at its source by re-anchoring the clue inside the target culture’s own idioms, keeping the internal logic seamless. V conveys the essential situational detail directly, bypassing wording while preserving the observation-and-inference core these stories reward.  

By contrast, II and VI insert external audio or phonetic scaffolding that fractures the closed, print-based deductive flow the plots rely on; readers must pause, consult outside material, and resume, undercutting the very immersion the mysteries expect. IV adds an extraneous accent never present in the originals, as neither story hinges on foreign pronunciation quirks or mishearing due to speech patterns—this retrofitting would convert the intended symbolic hinge into something incidental and unsupported by the source material. The viable practices therefore remain I-III-V, as this set respects both the translation obstacle and the conventions of ordinary literary adaptation. Colleagues favoring I-II-VI appear to over-weight isolated sound at the expense of the broader cultural resonance that actually supplies the twist.}

